When a method calls a library that throws a checked exception, the compiler insists you either catch it or declare it. Skipping both is a compile error.

```java
String read(String path) {
    // IOException is checked and not handled here
    return Files.readString(Path.of(path));  // compile error: unhandled IOException
}
```

Here both keywords appear in one method: `throw` fires an unchecked exception, while `throws` declares the checked one the method may propagate.

```java
// 'throws' declares the checked exception this method may propagate
String read(String path) throws IOException {
    if (path == null)
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("path required"); // 'throw' raises now (unchecked)
    return Files.readString(Path.of(path));                  // may propagate IOException
}
```
*`throw` fires the unchecked `IllegalArgumentException`; `throws` declares the checked `IOException`.*

This method declares a checked exception it can never raise, and the compiler accepts it. Every caller now pays for handling that can never fire.

```java
// Compiles fine even though nothing here throws IOException:
int addNumbers(int a, int b) throws java.io.IOException {
    return a + b;
}

// Every caller is now forced to handle an impossible exception:
void use() {
    try {
        int sum = addNumbers(2, 3);
    } catch (java.io.IOException e) {   // can never actually happen
        // dead code -- pure noise
    }
}
```
*Better: drop the `throws` clause so callers aren't burdened with handling that can never fire.*

Here the override declares a broader checked exception than the parent. The compiler rejects it because callers of the parent type were never prepared for it.

```java
class Reader {
    void read() throws IOException { }
}

class NetReader extends Reader {
    // Broader than parent's IOException -> compile error
    @Override
    void read() throws Exception { }   // 'Exception' is wider than 'IOException'
}
```

These overrides are all legal: a subtype, nothing at all, and unchecked exceptions thrown freely.

```java
class Reader {
    void read() throws IOException { }
}

class FileReaderImpl extends Reader {
    @Override
    void read() throws FileNotFoundException { }  // subtype of IOException: OK
}

class MemReader extends Reader {
    @Override
    void read() { }                               // throws nothing: also OK
    // It may still throw any unchecked RuntimeException freely.
}
```
*Narrowing or dropping checked exceptions is fine; widening or adding new ones is not.*

Here the new exception is thrown without the original as its cause. The real `SQLException` and its stack trace are discarded.

```java
try {
    repo.save(order);
} catch (SQLException e) {
    // Original SQLException and its stack trace are discarded
    throw new OrderSaveException("save failed");
}
```

These two forms both keep the root reachable — one rethrows the same instance, the other wraps while passing the cause.

```java
// (a) rethrow the same instance after logging
try {
    repo.save(order);
} catch (SQLException e) {
    metrics.increment("save.failure");
    throw e;                                   // unchanged, caller handles
}

// (b) wrap and rethrow, passing the cause
try {
    repo.save(order);
} catch (SQLException e) {
    throw new OrderSaveException("save failed", e);  // <-- cause preserved
}
```
*With the cause attached, logs show `Caused by: java.sql.SQLException ...` and the real stack trace.*

With the broadest possible declaration, the caller has no idea what can really fail and is pushed into a catch-all that also hides real bugs.

```java
// Caller has no idea what can really fail
public Report build() throws Exception {
    var data = fetch();   // IOException? SQLException? who knows
    return parse(data);
}

// Forced into a catch-all that also hides real bugs:
try { build(); }
catch (Exception e) { log.error("failed", e); }
```

Listing the precise checked exceptions makes the contract self-documenting and lets the caller respond to each failure differently.

```java
// Self-documenting: callers know exactly what to handle
public Report build() throws IOException, SQLException {
    var data = fetch();   // IOException
    return parse(data);   // SQLException
}

// Caller can respond to each case meaningfully:
try { build(); }
catch (IOException e)  { retryLater(); }
catch (SQLException e) { rollback(); }
```
*Specific types make the contract honest and let callers handle each failure differently.*

Here the original `SQLException` is dropped because the new exception is built without it as the cause. The root trace is gone.

```java
try {
    repo.save(order);
} catch (SQLException e) {
    throw new OrderException("save failed");   // cause dropped -> root trace gone
}
```

Both forms keep the original reachable — one rethrows the same instance, the other wraps it while passing the cause.

```java
// Rethrow same: observe, then let caller handle the original
try {
    repo.save(order);
} catch (SQLException e) {
    log.warn("save attempt failed for {}", order.id());
    throw e;
}

// Throw new at a boundary: translate, but pass the cause
try {
    repo.save(order);
} catch (SQLException e) {
    throw new OrderException("could not save order " + order.id(), e);
}
```
*Wrap only to add meaning at a boundary, and always chain the original as the cause.*
